          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            An
            %strong operad
            is a gadget used to describe algebraic structures in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric monoidal categories
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                a bunch of abstract
                %em operations
                of arbitrarily many arguments;
            %li
              %p equipped with a notion of how to compose these;
            %li
              %p
                subject to evident
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associativity"} associativity
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unitality"} unitality
                conditions.
          %p Just like a monoid can be seen as a single-object category, an operad is equivalently a single-object multicategory. Multicategories with multiple objects are also called colored operads.
          %p
            An
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+over+an+operad"} algebra over an operad
            is a concrete realization of these abstract operations: an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            equipped with
            = succeed "-ary" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \otimes \cdots \otimes A \to A
            as specified by the operad, subject to the composition relation as specified by the operad.
          %p
            This way an operad is like a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere+theory"} Lawvere theory
            in that it can be used to describe algebraic structures having finitary operations obeying equational laws. However, unlike Lawvere theories, operads can be defined internal to general
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+categories"} symmetric monoidal categories
            where the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor product
            might not be the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+product"} cartesian product
          %p
            The notion of operad (and allied notions such as
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/PROP"} PROP
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/club"} club
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multicategory"} multicategory
            and so on) come in many flavors. Originally used in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+topology"} algebraic topology
            to provide a systematic formalism for describing the internal operations which exist on iterated
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/loop+space"} loop space
          %h2#definition_in_components Definition in components
          %p
            The original definition is due to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+May"} Peter May
            and was given in his book (
            %a{:href => "#May1972"}> The Geometry of Iterated Loop Spaces
            ). It describes an operad as a collection of operations equipped with a notion of composition and subject to various conditions.
          %p
            This definition is essentially that of an
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category"} enriched category
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hom-object"} hom-object
          %p
            There is a more abstract way to encode all this simply as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid+in+a+monoidal+category"} monoid
            in a suitable ambient
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Conceptual"} A detailed conceptual treatment
          %h3#PedestrianDefOperad Symmetric operads
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            be a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric monoidal category
          %p#PermutativeOperad
            A (
            %em permutative
            or
            = succeed ")" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+operad"} symmetric
            %strong operad
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            consists of objects
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(n)
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            indexed over the
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+number"} natural number
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n = 0, 1, 2, \ldots
            (which we intuitively think of as “objects that parametrize the
            = succeed "-ary" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %ul
            %li
              Right
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/actions"} actions
              of
              = succeed "s" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+group"} symmetric group
              = succeed ";" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi ρ
                        %mi n
                      %mo :
                      %msub
                        %mi S
                        %mi n
                      %mo →
                      %mi hom
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ,
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho_n: S_n \to \hom(F(n), F(n))
            %li
              A
              %em unit
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi e
                    %mo :
                    %mi I
                    %mo →
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e: I \to F(1)
              [which we think of as picking out the identity map as unary operation];
            %li
              Composition operations
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi k
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi n
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi n
                        %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi ⋯
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi n
                        %mi k
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi n
                        %mn 1
                      %mo +
                      %mi …
                      %mo +
                      %msub
                        %mi n
                        %mi k
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(k) \otimes F(n_1) \otimes F(n_2) \otimes \cdots \otimes F(n_k) \to F(n_1 + \ldots + n_k)
              %p
                (which we think of as the result of plugging the outputs of operations
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi θ
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mi …
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi θ
                        %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \theta_1, \ldots, \theta_k
                into a
                = succeed "-ary" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi θ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \theta
                = succeed ")." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi θ
                        %mo ∘
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi θ
                          %mn 1
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mi …
                        %mo ⊗
                        %msub
                          %mi θ
                          %mi k
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \theta \circ (\theta_1 \otimes \ldots \otimes \theta_k)
          %p
            These data are subject to obvious identities such as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associativity"} associativity
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unitality"} unitality
            of composition, and compatibility of composition with symmetric group actions. For example, the unit laws say that the evident composite
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≅
                  %mi I
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %mi e
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mn 1
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi comp
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(n) \cong I \otimes F(n) \stackrel{e \otimes 1}{\to} F(1) \otimes F(n) \stackrel{comp}{\to} F(n)
          %p is the identity map, as is
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≅
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msup
                    %mi I
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi n
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ⊗
                      %msup
                        %mi e
                        %mrow
                          %mo ⊗
                          %mi n
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi n
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi comp
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(n) \cong F(n) \otimes I^{\otimes n} \stackrel{1 \otimes e^{\otimes n}}{\to} F(n) \otimes F(1)^{\otimes n} \stackrel{comp}{\to} F(n)
          %p
            Compatibility with symmetric group actions means that for each element
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma \in S_k
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msubsup
                    %mo ⨂
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                    %mi k
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi n
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi n
                    %mn 1
                  %mo +
                  %mi …
                  %mo +
                  %msub
                    %mi n
                    %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(k) \otimes \bigotimes_{i = 1}^k F(n_i) \to F(n_1 + \ldots + n_k)
          %p coequalizes a pair of automorphisms
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ρ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi σ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi λ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi σ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msubsup
                    %mo ⨂
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                    %mi k
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi n
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ⇉
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msubsup
                    %mo ⨂
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                    %mi k
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi n
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho(\sigma) \otimes 1, 1 \otimes \lambda(\sigma): F(k) \otimes \bigotimes_{i=1}^k F(n_i) \;\rightrightarrows\; F(k) \otimes \bigotimes_{i=1}^k F(n_i)
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            acts on the big tensor product on the left by permuting tensor factors in the obvious way. If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            has suitable colimits, this condition could be expressed in terms of tensor products over
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_n
          %p The associativity condition will be left for others to fill in.
          %h3#PedestrianDefColoredOperad Colored operads
          %p
            There is an evident generalization of the above where we allow the operad to have several
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
            %em colors
            in operad theory. This models algebraic structures where elements of different
            %em types
            may be fed into
            = succeed "-ary" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            be a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            %em set of colors
            \. Then a
            %strong colored operad
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            is
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                for each
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi ℕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \in \mathbb{N}
                and each
                = succeed "-tuple" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi n
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n+1)
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi c
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mi ⋯
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi c
                        %mi n
                      %mo ,
                      %mi c
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
